  <wpt lat="49.650379968250164" lon="-110.00419574603106">
    <name>End of Alberta Section of GNBR</name>
    <cmt>stop</cmt>
    <desc>Start of Sasketchewan section of GNBR</desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>generic</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="50.33158849462255" lon="-112.59353037032018">
    <name>Start of Canal Roads</name>
    <cmt>generic</cmt>
    <desc>A general rule when it comes to canal roads, which are car-free, is that they are engineered to be at nearly zero grade - the same as the canals themselves - and are well maintained. Some may have “No Trespassing” signs on them, but they are used by the public including gravel bike riders as a right of way. Occasionally, you may have to cross to the other side of canal to find better riding surfaces.</desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>generic</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="50.63537385348886" lon="-115.0210335933007">
    <name>Steep climb. Expect some pushing.</name>
    <cmt>caution</cmt>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>danger</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="50.90274000000001" lon="-114.66999">
    <name>Route Alternative</name>
    <cmt>control</cmt>
    <desc>For a paved option, riders can choose to stay on main road here towards Brant Creek and then take road 762 south to meet up with the route.</desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>checkpoint</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="50.67819990203513" lon="-114.97101140952176">
    <name>Big Elbow Trail</name>
    <cmt>trailhead</cmt>
    <desc>Some punchy ups and down. Caution on steep, chunky descents </desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>info</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.01143720864285" lon="-115.21834649002693">
